let __v_456 = pyrt::literals::decode("{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"soy sauce\",\"light soy\",\"dark soy\",\"shoyu\"]},\"Soy sauce keeps for years in the cupboard, and about a year once it is opened, because the salt stops anything growing in it. It darkens and a salt crust can form at the neck, and neither is spoilage; bin it only if it smells sour or has a film on top.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"vinegar\",\"malt vinegar\",\"white vinegar\",\"balsamic\",\"cider vinegar\",\"wine vinegar\"]},\"Vinegar does not go off: it keeps for years, opened or not, because it is already acid. A cloudy stringy blob in the bottle is the mother, which is harmless - strain it out and carry on - and a weaker taste after a couple of years is the only thing that changes.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"tea\",\"tea bags\",\"teabags\",\"tea bag\",\"loose leaf\",\"green tea\",\"earl grey\",\"pg tips\",\"chamomile tea\",\"peppermint tea\"]},\"Tea does not go off, it goes stale: about six months past the date in a sealed tin, and a year for loose leaf, after which the flavour fades rather than spoils. Keep it in a tin away from the light and the kettle's steam, and it is safe to drink long after it has lost its best.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"peanut butter\",\"peanut butter jar\",\"nut butter\",\"almond butter\"]},\"An opened jar of peanut butter keeps about three months in the cupboard, or six in the fridge if you want it to last. The oil that separates on top is normal - stir it back in rather than pouring it off - and the sign to bin it is a smell of paint or old oil, which is the nuts going rancid.\",\"Peanut butter freezes well: a jar keeps about six months in the freezer and thaws overnight in the fridge, though it may need stirring again afterwards.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"ham\",\"sliced ham\",\"cooked ham\",\"wafer thin ham\",\"honey roast ham\"]},\"An opened pack of cooked ham keeps two to three days in the fridge, sealed in a box, and the use-by date on the pack only holds while it is unopened. It should feel dry and smell of nothing much; sliminess or a sour smell means bin it, and do not re-wrap it and hope.\",\"Cooked ham freezes for about one to two months: wrap it in portions, because it goes watery and salty once thawed and will not slice thin again. Thaw it in the fridge overnight and use it in a cooked dish rather than a sandwich.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"ketchup\",\"tomato ketchup\",\"brown sauce\",\"hp sauce\"]},\"Opened ketchup keeps about a month in the cupboard and six months in the fridge, and the date on the bottle only counts while it is sealed. A dark colour or a little watery liquid is normal; a fizzy smell or mould at the neck means bin it.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"marmite\",\"vegemite\",\"bovril\"]},\"Marmite keeps for years - a couple of years past the date, easy - because it is salty and dark and nothing grows in it. It can dry out and go stiff in the jar; a spoonful of hot water stirred in brings it back, and it is safe to finish either way.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"golden syrup\",\"treacle\",\"black treacle\",\"maple syrup\"]},\"Golden syrup and treacle keep for years in the cupboard, and black treacle improves for the first year. Maple syrup is the one that needs the fridge once opened, about six months, and it is the only one here that can grow mould on top.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"worcestershire sauce\",\"worcester sauce\",\"tabasco\",\"hot sauce\",\"chilli sauce\",\"sriracha\",\"franks\"]},\"Worcestershire sauce and vinegar-based hot sauces keep for years in the cupboard, and about a year once opened; a hot sauce can darken and thicken, which is fine. The ones to refrigerate are the fresh chilli sauces and anything with fruit in it, which keep about three months.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"salt\",\"table salt\",\"sea salt\",\"rock salt\"]},\"Salt does not go off at all: it is a mineral and nothing grows in it, so the date means nothing. It clumps in damp air - a few grains of rice in the cellar, or a quick dry in a warm oven - and that is the only thing that changes.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"baking powder\",\"bicarbonate of soda\",\"bicarb\",\"baking soda\"]},\"Baking powder keeps about six months once the tin is opened, and bicarbonate of soda about a year, because they lose their fizz rather than spoil. Test it: a teaspoon in a cup of hot water should foam at once, and if it does not, the cake will not rise.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"cornflour\",\"corn flour\",\"custard powder\"]},\"Cornflour keeps about a year in a sealed tub in the cupboard, and custard powder about six months once opened, because both take up damp and go lumpy. It does not go mouldy or unsafe - it stops thickening properly, which is the sign to replace it.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"ice cream\",\"ice cream tub\",\"vanilla ice cream\",\"gelato\",\"sorbet\"]},\"Ice cream keeps about two to three months in the freezer before it dries out and goes icy, though it is safe far longer. Press a sheet of cling film onto the surface or keep the cardboard lid tight, and do not refreeze a tub that has melted, because that is when it turns grainy and grows ice crystals.\"]}]}")?;
