let __v_1193 = pyrt::literals::decode("{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"mango\",\"mangoes\",\"mangos\"]},\"A ripe mango gives slightly under a gentle press, like a ripe peach, and smells sweet at the stem end; the red blush says nothing, so a green one can still be ready.\",\"A hard one ripens on the counter in 2 to 4 days, faster in a paper bag with a banana, since the banana gives off the gas that does the work.\",\"Once it gives, it keeps about 5 days in the fridge, and a cut mango keeps 3 days in a sealed tub.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"melon\",\"melons\",\"cantaloupe\",\"honeydew\",\"galia\"]},\"A ripe melon feels heavy for its size, and the end opposite the stem gives a little under your thumb; a cantaloupe smells sweet there too.\",\"A whole one softens on the counter in 2 or 3 days, quicker in a paper bag, but once it is cut it will not ripen any further.\",\"Wrap cut melon and keep it 3 days in the fridge; a whole ripe one keeps about a week there.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"watermelon\",\"watermelons\"]},\"A ripe watermelon is heavy for its size, has a creamy yellow patch where it lay on the ground, and gives a hollow thud when you tap it.\",\"It will not ripen once it is off the vine, so a pale one stays pale and the only thing to do is keep it cool and eat it.\",\"Whole, it keeps a week or two in a cool room, and cut watermelon keeps 3 days in the fridge in a sealed tub.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"peach\",\"peaches\",\"nectarine\",\"nectarines\"]},\"A ripe peach gives to a gentle press and smells sweet at the stem, and the skin between the red patches is cream rather than green.\",\"Peaches ripen in a paper bag on the counter in 1 to 3 days, and the fridge is what stops them and turns them mealy.\",\"Once they give, keep them in the fridge for 3 to 5 days and take them out an hour before you eat them.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"plum\",\"plums\",\"apricot\",\"apricots\",\"greengage\",\"greengages\"]},\"A ripe plum gives slightly under the thumb and smells sweet at the stem, and its skin should be tight rather than wrinkled.\",\"Plums and apricots ripen in a paper bag on the counter in 2 to 4 days.\",\"Once ripe they keep about a week in the fridge, and 3 days in a fruit bowl.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"cherry\",\"cherries\"]},\"A ripe cherry is firm and shiny, dark all over for its variety, with a green stem that bends rather than snaps.\",\"Cherries do not ripen after picking, so a pale, soft one stays pale and soft.\",\"Keep them unwashed in the fridge for about a week and wash them just before you eat, because a wet cherry splits and moulds.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"pear\",\"pears\"]},\"A ripe pear gives a little at the neck, just under the stem, and a pear that is soft in the belly is already past its best.\",\"Pears ripen from the inside out, so buy them firm and leave them on the counter for 2 to 4 days, or in a paper bag with a banana.\",\"Once the neck gives, they keep about 5 days in the fridge and go floury left out in the bowl.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"kiwi\",\"kiwis\",\"kiwifruit\"]},\"A ripe kiwi gives under a gentle press, the way a ripe avocado does.\",\"Hard kiwis ripen on the counter in 3 to 5 days, faster in a paper bag with an apple or a banana.\",\"Once soft, they keep about 2 weeks in the fridge, and apart from other fruit if you want them to stay firm.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"pineapple\",\"pineapples\"]},\"A ripe pineapple smells sweet at the base, feels heavy for its size, and a leaf from the middle of the crown pulls out easily. Softening at the bottom is part of it ripening, so a firm one with a soft base is ready rather than wrong.\",\"A pineapple gets no sweeter once it is picked, only softer, and it starts at the base, so the one you buy is as good as it will get.\",\"Whole, it keeps about a week on the counter, and cut pineapple keeps 3 to 4 days in the fridge in its own juice. One that is soft all over and smells of wine is overripe: eat it today or freeze it.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"fig\",\"figs\"]},\"A ripe fig is soft all over, droops at the neck and often has a bead of syrup at the eye.\",\"Figs do not ripen once they are picked, so a hard one stays hard.\",\"Keep them 2 or 3 days in the fridge, laid on a plate rather than piled up, and they taste best at room temperature.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"grape\",\"grapes\",\"bunch of grapes\"]},\"Ripe grapes are firm and come away from the bunch easily, with a dusty bloom on the skin rather than a sticky one.\",\"Grapes do not ripen after picking, so a sour bunch stays sour.\",\"Keep them unwashed in a bag in the fridge for a week or more, and wash them just before eating.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"tomato\",\"tomatoes\"]},\"A tomato is ready when it feels heavy and gives slightly in the hand, smells of tomato where the stalk was, and the colour is deep and even; a hard, pale one that smells of green is not there yet.\",\"They ripen off the vine in a bowl on the counter, quicker with a banana or an apple beside them, and the fridge is what stops that and kills the flavour.\",\"Keep them out of the fridge for up to a week on the counter, and a cut tomato in a tub for 2 days.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"lemon\",\"lemons\",\"lime\",\"limes\"]},\"Citrus is picked ripe, so the test is weight rather than colour: a lemon or lime that feels heavy for its size and gives slightly under a thumb is full of juice, and a light, rock-hard one is dry inside.\",\"Nothing ripens them at home - a hard one softens a little on the counter in a few days, and a soft one goes straight in the fridge.\",\"Keep them a week or two in the bowl and a month in the fridge, and a lemon going soft is still good for juice and zest.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"orange\",\"oranges\",\"satsuma\",\"satsumas\",\"clementine\",\"clementines\",\"tangerine\",\"tangerines\"]},\"An orange or a satsuma is ready when it feels heavy for its size, which is juice rather than thick skin, and smells sweet at the stem; the small thin-skinned ones are usually the sweeter.\",\"Citrus does not ripen any further once it is picked, so a pale, dry one stays that way.\",\"Keep them a week or two in the bowl and a month in the fridge, and a clementine that is light and hollow-feeling has dried out at the ends.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"apple\",\"apples\",\"braeburn\",\"cox\",\"gala\",\"granny smith\"]},\"Apples are picked ripe and keep, so judge by feel: firm, heavy, a clean apple smell and a fresh green stem.\",\"There is nothing to ripen - a soft or mealy one has been off the tree too long and will not come back.\",\"Keep them for weeks in the fridge, far longer than on the counter, and away from other fruit because an apple gives off the gas that ripens everything beside it.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"coconut\",\"coconuts\"]},\"Shake it: a fresh coconut sloshes with liquid, and a silent one has dried out inside. Pick one heavy for its size with no cracks or damp patches at the eyes.\",\"A coconut is picked ripe, so there is nothing to do at home but open it.\",\"Drink the water the day it is opened, keep the flesh 3 to 4 days in a tub in the fridge, and freeze what you will not eat.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"papaya\",\"papayas\",\"pawpaw\",\"pawpaws\"]},\"A papaya is ready when the skin is mostly yellow-orange rather than green, it gives slightly under a thumb and smells faintly sweet rather than green.\",\"A green one ripens on the counter in 2 or 3 days, faster in a paper bag, and the fridge holds it once it is soft.\",\"Eat a ripe one within 2 days, and a cut one within 2 days from the fridge.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"strawberry\",\"strawberries\"]},\"Strawberries do not ripen after picking, so look for deep red right up to the tip under the leaves, a sweet smell and a dry, bright green cap.\",\"There is nothing to ripen at home and a pale one will not colour up; take them out of the fridge half an hour before you eat them, which is the only thing that improves them.\",\"Keep them 3 to 5 days in the fridge, unwashed and spread on kitchen paper, since washing them early is what turns them soft.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"blueberry\",\"blueberries\",\"raspberry\",\"raspberries\",\"blackberry\",\"blackberries\"]},\"Ripe berries are deep in colour all over with a dusty bloom, plump and only just soft, and a shrunken or leaking one is past it; taste one, because a punnet is better judged by flavour than by colour.\",\"Berries do not ripen once they are picked, so a pale one stays pale and sharp.\",\"Keep them 3 to 5 days in the fridge, spread on a plate lined with kitchen paper rather than heaped in a bag.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"pomegranate\",\"pomegranates\"]},\"A ripe pomegranate is heavy for its size, has square-ish sides rather than a round ball, and leathery, slightly scuffed skin rather than a shiny one.\",\"Pomegranates are picked ripe and do not ripen further, so a pale, light one stays sharp.\",\"Keep them for weeks in a cool cupboard, the seeds sweetest the day it is opened.\"]}]}")?;
